{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on exact d3506e02. Provenance and the Git bridge are now sound, but two substantive blockers remain. First, tools/call runs s.call and then asks s.attachedRoom() for the adjunct. attachedRoom prefers the adapter default repository and otherwise only returns a sole attachment. After calls attach more than one repository, a call with arguments.repo can therefore read live attention from the wrong default workroom or report unavailable despite having just acted in a known room. That can disclose another repository’s addressed inbox/focus and violates the every-completed-call condition. Make call return or retain the exact room selected for that invocation, including error paths, and use only that room for attention; add a two-attached-repository test proving each call reads its own room and never the default/other room. Second, eventIDPattern has no token boundaries, so attentionEvents extracts a canonical-looking substring from longer text such as xgit:sha1:\u003c40\u003e#git:sha1:\u003c40\u003ey. The docs promise exact canonical identifiers and no inference. Parse JSON string values or add strict boundaries and mutation-sensitive prefix/suffix tests. Preserve the current fail-soft envelope, actor aggregation, full fingerprint, heartbeat-age, boundedness, repetition-until-ack, and docs behavior. I found no separate architecture or simplification blocker: Nexus owns leases/matching, service owns the endpoint, and the MCP adapter owns adjunct shaping. Security review is otherwise sound, but the cross-repository selection bug is a privacy boundary defect. Re-run the requested mutations and full gates at a new exact head, publish replacement artifacts, report, and request fresh review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:832a9330cbf51a8faf849e1bcb605cb68fcc48e2","head":"d3506e02920dfc79d8b051ed06fe0e026722ca56","verdict":"changes-requested"}}